# MeTTa compatibility xfail list
# Format:  <relative-path>  <STATUS>:<CATEGORY>:<id>  [free-form notes]
# STATUS  ∈ COMPILE_FAIL | RUN_EXCEPTION | ASSERT_FAIL
# CATEGORY ∈ FEATURE_PENDING | STDLIB_GAP | SEMANTIC_DIVERGENCE | KNOWN_BUG
#
# A test is EXPECTED_FAIL when its actual STATUS matches the line.
# A mismatch (test fails for a different reason) is reported as REGRESSION.
# A test now passing is reported as UNEXPECTED_PASS — remove its line.
#
# The runner skips files imported by other files in the suite (modules).
# So entries here only need to cover files that run as standalone tests.

# f1_imports COMPILES and runs. The multivalued-tuple-lift blocker is FIXED: the first assert's
# `((let $x (get-atoms &self) (get-type $x)))` now lifts into
#   flat-map?(\$v. map?(\$e. ($e), ((\ ($x) (get-type $x)) $v)), (get-atoms &self))
# instead of freezing as an Atom[] holding a lambda. The `contains`/`is-space` and the first
# `import!` asserts pass too.
#
# :56 — the `(match &m (= (f 2) $x) $x)` = `(g 3)` assertion — now PASSES. It needed four things,
# all landed: the match result evaluated (`matchReduceDeep`), RUN-TIME visibility (a module's
# load-time `&self` rebound to the module's own space), COMPILE-TIME ordered visibility (a `!`-run
# resolves only against modules imported into `&self` before it), and applicative reduction of an
# inert application's arguments (`(g (+ 1 2))` -> `(g 3)`).
#
# :65 passes too, along with everything up to the diamond import. It had two independent
# pre-existing causes, both now fixed: the module compile queue was sorted by PATH (so f1_moduleA
# resolved before f1_moduleC and its `(g (+ 1 $x))` was emitted as inert data), and a computed
# argument reaching a parameter whose declared type merely ERASES to `Atom` was frozen by the
# meta-type rule (`(: g (-> Number Number))` got the un-reduced `(+ 1 2)`).
#
# The file now stops at **:114**, and the two later repeats of the same assertion:
#   !(assertEqualToResult (dup 2) (12 102))
# `dup` is defined in BOTH imported modules — f1_moduleA's answers `(g 2)` = 102, f1_moduleB's
# answers `(+ 2 10)` = 12 — and hyperon UNIONS them: a name declared in several imported modules
# is non-deterministic. JeTTa keeps one owner per name (`Context.resolvedFunctions` is a flat
# name -> SymbolDef map, last writer wins — here f1_moduleB, hence the actual `[12]`), so the
# call compiles to a single static invocation.
#
# What it needs: a call whose name has more than one VISIBLE owner must dispatch over all of them
# and union the results, i.e. become multivalued. The visible-owner set is already computed per
# `!`-run for ordered visibility (Context.visibleOwnersAt), so the information is in place; what
# is missing is a multi-owner symbol table and a bag-returning call shape for that case.
f1_imports.metta            ASSERT_FAIL:FEATURE_PENDING:multi-module-same-name-nondeterminism    :114 `(dup 2)` -> [12], expected [12 102]; one owner kept per name. See notes above
